WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.480
He is remembered as a military genius, a tactician
without peer.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:08.069
At the age of 35, having just crowned himself
the First Emperor of France, he set about

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:10.750
conquering all of Europe.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:15.690
His name was Napoleon Bonaparte and he was,
above all else, a master propagandist.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.900
He made use of his extraordinary way with
words to publish political manifestos, newspapers,

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.380
and, later, his autobiography.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:27.380
He regularly commissioned portraits and sculptures
and was acutely aware of how to present himself

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:28.630
in the best light.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:33.110
Even when he made moves to crown himself Emperor,
he used propaganda and political nouse to

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:36.230
convince the populace that it was their idea.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:40.650
Napoleon has always aroused one of two emotions;
hatred or loyalty.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.730
Everything around him seemed bigger than life.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:47.320
Yet, he had none of the physical traits that
we associate with power – he was short (five

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:52.890
foot, two), had extremely pale skin, small
delicate hands and a large head set on a short,

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:53.890
stout neck.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:58.579
Despite this, his inner self-belief projected
the aura of invincibility that made him a

00:00:58.579 --> 00:00:59.930
natural leader of men.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:04.820
It led him to the dizzying heights of victory
– and the crushing despair of defeat.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:08.799
In this week’s Biographix, we discover the
real Napoleon Bonaparte . . .

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:30.270
“ I found the crown of France in the gutter,
and I picked it up.”

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.270
Early Life

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:33.939
In the Spring of 1769, the tiny island nation
of Corsica was under siege.

00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:39.030
A hardy band of Corsican patriots were determined
to repel the French army that had invaded

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:40.030
their land.

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:41.090
They never stood a chance.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:45.020
After a year of fighting, death and carnage
the rebels were defeated.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.049
The survivors trudged through the mountains
back to their homes.

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:50.479
Among them were Letitzia and Carlo Bonaparte.

00:01:50.479 --> 00:01:52.380
Letitzia was six months pregnant.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:57.380
It was the Day of the Feast of Assumption,
August 15th, 1769 when Letitzia gave birth

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.149
to her second child.

00:01:59.149 --> 00:02:00.479
He was named Napoleon.

00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:05.009
Napoleon’s father, Carlo, gave up the flag
of Corsican independence once French victory

00:02:05.009 --> 00:02:06.009
was assured.

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:09.360
He refused to fight on in the mountains with
his former patriot hold outs.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.690
The 23- year-old university student ingratiated
himself to the French overlords and quickly

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.250
took on the trappings of French privilege.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:19.790
It was something that his second son would
never forgive him for, viewing his father

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.830
as a traitor who had betrayed his countrymen.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:24.700
Carlo entered upon a law practice.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.440
Before long he had won election to the Corsican
Assembly.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.420
He became accepted among the elite of Corsican
society and was viewed with favor by the French

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:33.420
rulers.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.630
Yet, the more successful he became, the more
it drove a wedge between he and his second

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:38.630
son.

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:42.080
In contrast to how he viewed his father, the
young Napoleon simply adored his mother.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.120
Letitzia was a strong willed, hardy woman.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.062
Of her 13 pregnancies, she ended up with 8
children.

00:02:46.062 --> 00:02:49.890
A harsh disciplinarian, she would tolerate
no nonsense.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:54.570
Yet, Napoleon would later reminisce that all
of his success in life was due to the training

00:02:54.570 --> 00:03:01.530
that he had received at his mother’s knee.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:07.130
Military Academy

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:11.430
Napoleon set foot on the French mainland for
the first time in the winter of 1778, his

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:15.900
father having secured him a position aat the
prestigious Royal Military College at Brienne

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.400
in Northern France.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.710
He hated it.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:23.810
Accustomed to the warmth of the Mediterranean,
the harsh climate shivered him to his bones.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:28.730
To top it off, the skinny, uncoordinated country
boy could hardly speak any French.

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:32.490
Any one of the facts that he was a scholarship
boy, a country bumpkin and a loyal Corsican

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:36.100
with a strange accent would have been enough
to make him the target of bullies.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:39.550
Yet, somehow, Napoleon accustomed himself
to life at Brienne.

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:43.640
His stubbornness and tough exterior made him
immune to the taunts of his more privileged

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:44.640
classmates.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.750
Soon they, along with his teachers, lost interest
in the sullen, always serious Bonaparte.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:50.420
Napoleon became a loner.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:54.880
At the age of 16, Napoleon began his military
apprenticeship as a second lieutenant, training

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.680
with the country’s most elite artillery
unit.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:02.670
He learnt his trade well and developed into
a skilled handler of arms and a capable handler

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:03.670
of men.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:07.760
Yet, the advancement that he had mapped out
for himself was slow in coming.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.850
He soon realized that hard work and application
were not enough to forge a stellar military

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:16.690
career; position and money also played their
part.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:17.690
He had neither.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:21.410
By his late teens, Napoleon found himself
in a state of frustrated inertia.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:23.200
He knew that he was destined for great things.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.150
Yet nothing was happening for him.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:27.270
He was bored to death.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:32.540
What Napoleon needed was a cataclysmic event
that would upend the existing order and present

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:36.030
him with the opportunity to grab hold of and
shape the future.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:50.090
Little did he know that it was waiting just
around 

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:51.090
the corner.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:52.090
Paris in Flames

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:53.430
On July 14, 1789 Paris erupted in revolution.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:57.270
The spark that unleashed the revolution in
all it’s fury occurred when a crowd took

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:02.070
over an armory called Hotel des Invalides,
grabbed it’s weaponry and stormed the Bastille

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.570
prison in search of powder and shot.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:08.750
Word spread of the crowd’s success and uprisings
began all over France, with granaries and

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:10.480
manor houses overrun.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.770
The country was in turmoil.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:16.650
Twenty-year-old Napoleon was far from Paris
when revolution broke forth.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:20.870
Though not a revolutionary himself, he welcomed
the revolt which was shaking the system of

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:22.430
privileges which he so despised.

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:26.010
After all, it was this very system which had
prevented him from attaining to the advancement

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.820
of rank that he felt was his due.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:33.110
When the French Republic was declared in the
Fall of 1792, the ambitious Corsican wanted

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:34.610
to play a part.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:38.300
Formulating a plan, he took leave of absence
from the army and returned to Corsica, which

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:39.830
was now a part of France.

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:44.000
He was intent on entering local politics and
rising through the ranks until he could assert

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.280
his influence on the national stage.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:51.730
The Corsican governor, however, rebuffed Napoleon,
calling him ‘a big, inexperienced boy’.

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:55.720
Napoleon reacted by setting up a power base
to oppose the Governor.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.000
He attracted a following of young, impetuous
cronies.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.620
But the establishment proved too strong.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.970
Napoleon was forced to flee to the mountains.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:08.080
He and his entire family were labelled traitors
and enemies of the fatherland.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:13.261
Setting sail for France in June, 1793, Napoleon
was tougher, more steely eyed and more hard

00:06:13.261 --> 00:06:17.169
skinned as a result of his unhappy experience
in Corsica.

00:06:17.169 --> 00:06:22.550
The experience also caused a mental displacement;
no longer a Corsican he was now thoroughly

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:23.550
French.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:33.710
That France, however, was going to have to
be molded to make it worthy of him.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:38.389
Taking Control

00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:41.200
Napoleon returned to the army as an artillery
captain.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:46.320
He was quickly ordered to Toulon, a city of
28,000 which had broken into open rebellion.

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.850
The citizens of this southern port city had
thrown their port open to the British.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:54.020
It was the French artillery’s job to prevent
the British from gaining a foothold – and

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:55.650
to subjugate the rebels.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:59.680
The problem was that the British were defending
the city from their ships’ cannons.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:04.020
Napoleon knew that he had been presented with
the opportunity that could transform his destiny.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:09.110
Aristocratic officers had fled France, leaving
a vacuum that Napoleon was all too eager to

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:10.110
fill.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:11.919
All he had to do was prove himself.

00:07:11.919 --> 00:07:16.070
On his command, guns and supplies were rushed
in from Provence, and his gunners were taught

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:17.070
to man them.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:21.290
The regular infantry were intensely retrained
under Bonaparte’s direct supervision.

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:26.449
He also began to display flashes of the charisma
which would forge an unbreakable loyalty within

00:07:26.449 --> 00:07:27.460
his ranks.

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:31.630
On December 17, he personally led the assault
of Fort Eguilette.

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:34.350
His bravery and cunning were immediately apparent.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:37.210
Throwing himself into the fray, he fought
with fury.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:39.229
Unlike most commanders, Napoleon led from
the front.

00:07:39.229 --> 00:07:43.180
A horse was shot from under him and an enemy
bayonet slashed him in the thigh.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:47.290
Yet, he continued to attack and his forces
quickly overcame the resistance.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:51.510
Within hours, 10 British ships had been destroyed
and those that remained were in retreat.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:53.680
The local people were subjugated.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:58.350
It was a stunning victory for Napoleon, one
which led to a promotion to Brigadier General.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:03.300
In the spring of 1795, Napoleon visited Paris,
his ambition burning brighter than ever.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:07.500
While there, he was charged with subjugating
the Parisian mobs by any means necessary.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:12.660
He jumped at the opportunity, gathering cannon
and musket to equip his poorly armed force.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:16.200
He set his guns up inside the Tuileries in
positions that made it impregnable.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:21.060
When the attack came, Napoleon commanded his
men to unleash a merciless hail of fire upon

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:22.060
them.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:25.400
Waiting until he could see the whites of their
eyes, he sent a barrage of grapeshot that

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:27.310
decimated the Parisian mob.

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:28.470
“The enemy attacked us.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:29.750
We killed a great many of them.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:30.910
Now, all is quiet.

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:34.010
I could not be happier,” Bonaparte wrote
to his brother.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:35.920
Napoleon was triumphant.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:38.120
Within a month he had been made a full general.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:44.190
At just 26 years of age he was the Commander
of the Army of the Interior.

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:52.540
Victory After Victory

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:56.620
As a reward for his success against the Parisian
mobs, Napoleon was given command of the French

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:57.720
army in Italy.

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:02.399
There he was charged with defeating France’s
enemies, the Austrians, along with their Italian

00:09:02.399 --> 00:09:05.170
allies, and push them back over the Alps.

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:09.010
This was his first opportunity to fight on
foreign soil and Napoleon was determined to

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:10.290
capitalize on the opportunity.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:14.420
Prior to his arrival, Napoleon’s generals
had already written him off as a no account

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:16.649
upstart with allusions of grandeur.

00:09:16.649 --> 00:09:20.540
When Bonaparte arrived in the flesh, his generals
quickly realized their mistake.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:23.190
They had vastly underestimated their new commander.

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:24.730
He quickly put them in their place.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:29.000
For nearly two years their incompetence had
seen the French forces stagnating in the Alps

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:33.130
with nothing to show for it but a general
sense of apathy and discontent.

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:36.270
As the generals but their lips, Napoleon addressed
the troops . . .

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:39.050
“Soldiers, you are naked and ill fed.

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:40.529
No fame shines upon you.

00:09:40.529 --> 00:09:45.680
I will lead you into the most fertile plains
in the world; rich provinces and great cities

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:47.350
will lie in your power.

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:50.770
You will find there honor, glory and riches.

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:53.120
The men were enraptured by their new leader.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:57.120
Smiling and laughing in their midst, he was
unlike anything they had seen before.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.130
He picked up their spirits and made them believe
in themselves.

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:05.420
Infusing them with passion, he began molding
them into a formidable fighting force.

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:09.850
Meanwhile, the other European powers had become
very nervous about what was going on in France.

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:13.830
The execution of their king and queen led
to fears that the French experiment in democracy

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:16.550
would spread and threaten their own kingdoms.

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:19.750
This led them to quickly move against the
new French government.

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:24.290
This led to a series of wars between France
and most of the major European powers.

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:29.070
Napoleon built his army into the most efficient
conquering force on the European mainland.

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:34.300
Over the next three years he brought stunning
victories not only in Italy, but as far as

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.560
Austria and parts of North Africa.

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:40.800
His North African invasion was actually a
move against Britain, which used Egypt as

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:42.030
a trading route.

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:47.000
Though he failed to bring Egypt under Control,
Napoleon was greeted as a hero on his return

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:48.580
to Paris in 1799.

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:51.320
Yet, he faced a nation in turmoil.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:55.220
The government had no money, the Austrians
and the Russians were threatening to invade

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:58.060
and some people even wanted to bring back
the royal family.

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:00.430
Rumors of a coup were rife.

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:04.310
With the people and the army behind him, Napoleon
took charge of the Grand Assembly, installing

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:08.279
a new government that was to be headed by
three provisional Consuls.

00:11:08.279 --> 00:11:11.470
One of those three men was Napoleon Bonaparte
himself.

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:15.240
And it was he who everyone knew was going
to wield the power.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:16.570
Immediately Napoleon took control.

00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:20.270
He adroitly sidelined the other two Consuls,
personally rewriting the constitution to make

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:21.600
himself First Consul.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:26.580
At 30 years of age, Napoleon Bonaparte had
risen from his humble Corsican background

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:34.390
to become the most powerful man in France.

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:40.280
Absolute Power

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:44.670
As the absolute central power in France, Napoleon
had inherited a nation that had limped from

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:47.620
one failed attempt to replace the rule of
monarchy to another.

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:51.680
For many people, the conditions they found
themselves in at the dawn of the 19th century

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:53.870
were worse than before the Revolution.

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:56.860
They looked to Napoleon to save them from
their despair.

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:59.560
First, though, he had to save them from the
Austrians.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:04.100
The Austrians had regrouped and gained back
most of the land that Napoleon had secured

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:05.980
during the Italian campaign.

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:11.350
The Archduke, Francis II, was intent on cutting
down the Corsican usurper who thought he could

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:12.610
do as he pleased.

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:15.040
As was his custom, Napoleon decided to take
the offensive.

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:20.420
In the Spring of 1800 he took his 40,000 strong
army across the French Alps.

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:24.850
It was a harrowing trek, his men dragging
field artillery and provisions up steep, snow

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:28.570
covered mountain ranges which towered to a
height of 10,500 feet.

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:33.130
Men died en route and, as the Grand Armee
stumbled down through the St Bernard Pass

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:35.480
they were in a state of utter exhaustion.

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:40.050
They had made the crossing in just six days.

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:41.709
Battle was joined on June 14th.

00:12:41.709 --> 00:12:45.450
At day’s end 7,000 French men lay dead.

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:50.710
The Austrians, who had finally retreated from
the field of battle, left 14,000 bodies behind.

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:53.290
The totally spent Grand Armee had won again.

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:57.130
The Austrians withdrew and, early the next
year, signed a peace treaty with Napoleon.

00:12:57.130 --> 00:13:00.400
Great Britain was the only nation that could
stand in Napoleon’s way.

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:04.240
He was the supreme ruler of the land mass
that was Europe.

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:07.620
However, Great Britain had unquestioned command
of the seas.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:12.610
Fabulously rich due to her colonial conquests
and boasting the greatest navy in the world,

00:13:12.610 --> 00:13:16.030
Britain decided it was time to halt the rise
of the Little General.

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:20.399
She declared war on France on May 18, 1803.

00:13:20.399 --> 00:13:23.029
Napoleon decided it was time to invade England.

00:13:23.029 --> 00:13:26.399
Displaying the laser like focus that epitomized
the man, he stated,

00:13:26.399 --> 00:13:28.660
“All my thoughts are directed towards England.

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:34.149
I want only for a favorable wind to plant
the Imperial Eagle on the Tower of London.”

00:13:34.149 --> 00:13:38.240
As it turned out, the French invasion of Great
Britain never materialised.

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:43.280
By September, 1805 war with Austria, now fortified
by the Russians, was once again a priority.

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:47.001
The Grand Armee was being rushed from the
Coast to march into the heart of the Austrian

00:13:47.001 --> 00:13:48.001
Empire.

00:13:48.001 --> 00:13:52.851
Then, on October 21, the Royal Navy smashed
the combine Spanish-British fleet in the Battle

00:13:52.851 --> 00:13:56.420
of Trafalgar, leaving the French essentially
without a navy.

00:13:56.420 --> 00:14:00.110
The British were safe – for now.

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:03.130
In December 2nd, 1804 Napoleon had himself
crowned as the first Emperor of the French

00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:04.779
in a lavish ceremony.

00:14:04.779 --> 00:14:09.899
In all but name and place it was just like
the coronations of the old kings, with one

00:14:09.899 --> 00:14:10.899
telling difference.

00:14:10.899 --> 00:14:15.580
Napoleon, the controller of his own fate,
placed the crown, all 80 jewel encrusted pounds

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:24.410
of it, upon his own head!

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:28.250
Austerlitz

00:14:28.250 --> 00:14:32.641
On December 2nd, 1805 Napoleon found himself
again poised for battle against Austria, this

00:14:32.641 --> 00:14:34.420
time fortified by Russia.

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:37.740
It would prove to be the Little General’s
greatest victory.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:41.581
He deliberately abandoned a strategic position
near the town of Austerlitiz in the Austrian

00:14:41.581 --> 00:14:46.270
Empire so that his army, which numbered around
68,000, would appear vulnerable.

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:51.171
He then weakened his right flank so as to
lure the 90,000-strong foe into a trap.

00:14:51.171 --> 00:14:55.830
They left their center open to counterattack
and Napoleon cut their line in two.

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:59.310
By 4:30pm the Allies were either dead or far
in retreat.

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:01.590
The battlefield had fallen silent.

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:04.899
The Battle of Austerlitz was over.

00:15:04.899 --> 00:15:09.100
What had begun as a great allied opportunity
to finally defeat and destroy Napoleon Bonaparte

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:12.769
had ended, not only in their own defeat, but
in catastrophe.

00:15:12.769 --> 00:15:16.810
The grand victory at Austerlitz was followed
up with a 21-day subjugation of Prussia.

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:21.610
Over the next five years, victory followed
victory as, one by one, the European powers

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:24.670
bowed to the dictates of Emperor Napoleon.

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:30.670
Only one European nation remained to be brought
to heel – Russia.

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:39.370
The Russian Campaign

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:45.120
Napoleon and a colossal army crossed the Neman
River on June 24th 1812 to intimidate Russia,

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:47.680
but it turned out to be the undoing of his
empire.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:51.850
The Russians systematically retreated and
scorched the earth, which dragged the French

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:53.130
deep into their territory.

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:57.130
Then, when they did did do battle, it was
the bloodiest day of Napoleon’s career.

00:15:57.130 --> 00:16:00.680
The French entered Moscow a week later, only
to find it evacuated.

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:03.600
The retreat ended up being even more costly.

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:06.940
Soldiers had insufficient clothing for the
freezing temperatures of an early winter,

00:16:06.940 --> 00:16:11.000
disease devastated the ranks, and Russian
forces pursued them all the way.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:16.640
A little over a sixth of the 600,000 men who
marched into Russia came back.

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:20.079
It was a blow from which the Little General
would never recover.

00:16:20.079 --> 00:16:22.320
By now the political map of Europe had changed.

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:26.329
The British, Spanish and Portuguese pushed
the French back over the Pyrenees in the peninsular

00:16:26.329 --> 00:16:27.329
War.

00:16:27.329 --> 00:16:31.050
A grand coalition was formed, with the decisive
victory coming at the Battle of Liepzig in

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:32.649
October 1813.

00:16:32.649 --> 00:16:42.840
Known as the Battle of the Nations, it left
38,000 French dead or wounded and 20,000 captured.

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:49.000
Exile &amp; Return

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:52.570
Napoleon escaped only to find open hostility
back in Paris.

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:56.040
The Legislative Assembly, the Senate and even
his own generals turned on him.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:59.970
He had no choice but to abdicate, which he
did on April 6, 1840.

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:05.039
It was agreed to send Napoleon into exile
on the Mediterranean island of Elba.

00:17:05.039 --> 00:17:07.520
After ten months of exile, Napoleon had had
enough.

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:11.920
Somehow, on the night of February 26, 1815,
he managed to sneak past his guards and set

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:14.189
sail in a small boat for the mother country.

00:17:14.189 --> 00:17:17.240
With him were a handful of loyal soldiers.

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:19.809
Miraculously, they managed to elude British
warships.

00:17:19.809 --> 00:17:24.890
Stepping foot back on French soil, he fixed
his sights on Paris and taking back what had

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:26.230
been torn from him.

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:30.180
After six days, he and his few men were halted
by an infantry regiment with strict orders

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:31.309
to detain him.

00:17:31.309 --> 00:17:34.240
Napoleon, strode ahead of his followers and
stood in their midst . . .

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:39.540
Soldiers, he declared, if there is one among
you who wants to kill his general and emperor,

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:41.049
here I am.

00:17:41.049 --> 00:17:42.910
His charisma had done it again.

00:17:42.910 --> 00:17:47.140
Rather than rush to take him captive, the
massed infantry broke into wild applause.

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:49.270
Cheers of “long live the emperor!”

00:17:49.270 --> 00:17:50.270
filled the air.

00:17:50.270 --> 00:17:52.210
Suddenly he had an army.

00:17:52.210 --> 00:17:55.510
As he closed in on Paris, he drew more and
more support.

00:17:55.510 --> 00:17:59.630
In one place after another, troops defected
from the Royal army and joined the rebel army.

00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:01.630
The writing was on the wall.

00:18:01.630 --> 00:18:04.200
By the time Napoleon rode into Paris, Louis
XVIII had fled.

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:05.890
Napoleon was back in command.

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:13.770
The people welcomed him as a redeemed hero.

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:19.460
Downfall

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:22.010
Across Europe, the Allies were in shock and
disbelief.

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:24.160
The devil had been unchained.

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:26.660
They would have to unite once more to stop
him.

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:31.450
This time, though, they would have to totally
destroy the man, so that there was no coming

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:32.450
back.

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:36.370
Napoleon was declared an outlaw and a disturber
of the tranquility of the world.

00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:38.600
They massed their armies for attack.

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:39.919
The great reckoning was at hand.

00:18:39.919 --> 00:18:44.169
It would be delivered on a field of rye and
clover just outside of a small Belgian town

00:18:44.169 --> 00:18:45.610
called Waterloo.

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:51.360
On June 18th, 72,000 French soldiers faced
a 68,000-strong allied force under the Duke

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:52.549
of Wellington.

00:18:52.549 --> 00:18:57.620
While the fighting seemed even, Napoleon made
tactical errors, including launching his Imperial

00:18:57.620 --> 00:18:59.040
Guard too late.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:03.360
His most fatal error was to wait until midday
before ordering his initial attack in order

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:05.350
to let the muddy ground dry.

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:07.970
This gave the Prussian’s time to enter the
fray later on.

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:11.640
They smashed against his right flank and the
battle was lost.

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:15.990
Four days later, Napoleon abdicated again
– this time there would be no return.

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:19.160
He was packed off to the remote British Colony
of Saint Helena.

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:22.630
At 46, Napoleon was a man without a future.

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:27.160
The man of unstoppable ambition, the man of
action, the maker of headlines was confined

00:19:27.160 --> 00:19:29.450
to reading the newspaper and gardening.

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:33.540
He died on May 5th, 1821 likely from stomach
cancer.

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:36.710
Days before, when he knew that his time was
up, he had whispered to one of his guards

00:19:36.710 --> 00:19:37.710
. . .

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:38.710
To die is nothing.

00:19:38.710 --> 00:20:07.169
But, to live defeated and without glory is
to die 

00:20:07.169 --> 00:20:15.720
every day.

